---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PatchPreview from '../../components/PatchPreview.astro';
import { patchpilotGithub } from '../../data/projects.mjs';
const root = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title="PatchPilot AI — Harikrishna Trivedi" description="Supervised software repair agents: a scoped patch, executable evidence, and a human review checkpoint.">
  <section class="case-hero shell"><a href={`${root}#work`} class="back-link">← Back to selected work</a><div class="case-category">AI AGENTS / DEVELOPER TOOLS / 2026</div><h1>PatchPilot AI<span>.</span></h1><p class="case-deck">A patch is a proposal.<br/><em>The evidence makes the case.</em></p><div class="case-actions"><a class="primary-link" href={patchpilotGithub} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="primary-link" href="#repair-loop">Explore the workflow <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div><div class="case-facts"><div><span>PROJECT</span><p>Independent portfolio project</p></div><div><span>MY FOCUS</span><p>Scope, agent workflow & human review</p></div><div><span>STACK</span><p>Python · FastAPI · React · TypeScript · SQLite</p></div><div><span>STATUS</span><p>Local engineering prototype · v0.2</p></div></div></section>
  <div class="shell" data-reveal><figure class="project-stage case-main-stage patch-stage"><div class="stage-corner">A SCOPED CHANGE. AN INSPECTABLE RESULT.</div><PatchPreview/><figcaption>Interface illustration, not an application screenshot</figcaption></figure></div>
  <section class="case-section shell"><div class="case-section-label">01 / THE QUESTION</div><div class="case-copy" data-reveal><h2>Can a repair agent show its work<br/>before anyone trusts the patch?</h2><p>PatchPilot takes a reported bug in a small Python repository and produces a proposed source patch, a regression test, an execution trace, and a human review checkpoint. The goal is to make the reasoning and verification inspectable.</p><p>A developer chooses the allowed files, describes the expected behavior, and sets attempt and token budgets. Model roles propose changes; deterministic tools enforce scope and check the evidence.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-chapters shell" id="repair-loop"><div class="section-topline" data-reveal><span class="section-index">02 / THE REPAIR LOOP</span><span class="section-aside">From a reported issue to a reviewable handoff.</span></div><div class="case-chapter-grid">
    <article data-reveal><span class="chapter-number">01</span><h3>Give it a boundary.</h3><p>Import source and tests, write acceptance criteria, select editable files, and set budgets. The planner turns that request into a structured plan.</p><div class="mini-query">Issue → Scope → Plan <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div></article>
    <article data-reveal data-reveal-order="1"><span class="chapter-number">02</span><h3>Make the failure visible.</h3><p>The implementer proposes edits and a regression test. Verification checks the original failure and the patched suite; failed evidence guides a bounded retry.</p><div class="mini-evidence"><span aria-hidden="true">↻</span> Original fails · Patched passes</div></article>
    <article data-reveal data-reveal-order="2"><span class="chapter-number">03</span><h3>Hand back the decision.</h3><p>The reviewer assesses the proposal. A developer inspects the diff, trace, and concerns, then approves or rejects the handoff and exports the patch.</p><div class="mini-source"><i aria-hidden="true">↗</i><span>Diff · Tests · Trace<br/><strong>Human review stays in the loop.</strong></span></div></article>
  </div></section>
  <section class="case-section shell"><div class="case-section-label">03 / ENGINEERING NOTES</div><div class="case-copy" data-reveal><h2>Useful autonomy<br/>needs explicit boundaries.</h2><div class="engineering-row"><h3>Three model roles. One state machine.</h3><p>Planner, implementer, and reviewer have separate instructions and calls. A Python state machine coordinates them; deterministic verification enforces the test gates. Agents do not receive an arbitrary shell.</p></div><div class="engineering-row"><h3>Evidence before handoff</h3><p>The new regression must fail with an assertion on the original source, the patched suite must pass, existing tests must remain unchanged, and the reviewer must recommend approval. Human approval is recorded separately.</p></div><div class="engineering-row"><h3>Different execution modes</h3><p>The browser workspace prepares requests and inspects exported evidence. The local scripted demo runs known fixture repairs. Live repair uses a server-side model key and Docker prerequisites; it fails closed when they are missing.</p></div><div class="engineering-row"><h3>A deliberately narrow first version</h3><p>The supported unit is a small, dependency-free Python module with unittest tests. The prototype never applies, pushes, or merges a patch automatically. Live model and actual Docker execution were not verified in the authoring environment.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="case-reflection shell" data-reveal><span class="section-index">04 / HOW IT WAS BUILT</span><h2>AI helped write the system.<br/><span>Evidence shaped the workflow.</span></h2><p>I used AI-assisted implementation with human supervision to develop the interface, typed contracts, repair loop, and evaluation fixtures. Scripted examples exercise success, retry, and refusal; they are orchestration checks, not a measure of general model repair accuracy.</p><div class="case-actions"><a href={patchpilotGithub} class="primary-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the source <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href={`${root}work/carecontext/`} class="primary-link">Next: CareContext <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>
</Base>
